      "name": null,
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{630}^{∘})=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{630}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{630}^{∘}+{360}^{∘} & =-{270}^{∘} \\\\ -{630}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{630}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘}).$\n\nThe angle ${90}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266176/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘})=1.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{630}^{∘})=1.$",
