      "dom_id": "question-23422",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{360}^{∘})=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{360}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$-{360}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={0}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{360}^{∘})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({0}^{∘}).$\n\nThe angle ${0}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266176/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${0}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{∘}=1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{0}^{∘}=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{0}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{∘}}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{360}^{∘})=0.$",
